{:edb/db-before-t 1591 :edb/db-after-t 1592 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534904 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:39:33.795Z" 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117862 13194139534904 17592186045417 false] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117863 13194139534904 17592186045417 false] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117864 13194139534904 17592186045417 false] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117865 13194139534904 17592186045417 false] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117866 13194139534904 17592186045417 false] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443490 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443502 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443513 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443515 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186117861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443524 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186117861 1078 #edb/ref 17592186443524 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186119745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443543 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186119745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443544 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186119745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443548 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186119746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443547 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186119746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443549 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186119747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443542 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186119747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443545 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186119747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443546 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186120037 1050 900.0 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186120287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186120287 1071 "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t) = ⟨3t, 2\\sin (2t), 2\\cos (2t)⟩$, $t \\ge 0$, by its arc length $s$?" 13194139534904 17592186045417 false] [17592186120287 1071 "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186120287 1074 "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16({cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5u\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=5t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{5}) \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot \\frac{s}{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{5}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3s}{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186121001 1064 "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{'}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}u+{sin}^{2}u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n- Compute $s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u$\n\n- Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n- Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$" 13194139534904 17592186045417 false] [17592186121001 1064 "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡u+{sin}^{2}⁡u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Compute $s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u$\n\n2\\. Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n3\\. Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$" 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186443489 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})" 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186443489 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186443489 1083 #uuid "0078bb74-f290-5ea7-a214-52b9d695f5aa" 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186443490 1080 "R(s)=⟨\\frac{3s}{10},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})⟩" 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186443490 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186443490 1083 #uuid "03d35f72-203f-50ad-acf8-60daf8ec86f4" 13194139534904 17592186045417 true] [17592186443491 1080 "R(s)=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}),\\,2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})⟩" 13194139534904 1759218604